# DTF tier manifest: proxy-metadata-budget-env  (artifact-keeper#4881)
# Profile-set: filesystem + limits.proxy-metadata-budget (32 GiB budget,
# json-file log driver).
#
# THE BUG (#4881)
# ProxyMetadataBudget counted one tokio semaphore permit per byte and clamped
# its total to u32::MAX, because a reservation goes through acquire_many(u32).
# proxy_metadata_budget() applied the clamp with no log line, so an operator
# who set AK_PROXY_METADATA_BUDGET_BYTES=34359738368 (32 GiB) got a
# 4294967295-byte budget, and the conda virtual merge gate, which derives its
# default max_merges from that total (budget / (128 MiB fetch + 1 GiB decoded
# member ceilings), at least 2), stayed at 3. The fix counts permits in 4 KiB
# units, so the budget can express any usize.
#
# THE PRIMITIVE
# The merge gate is built lazily, on the first virtual conda merge, and logs
# one line: "conda virtual merge gate configured" with max_merges and
# budget_bytes. The oracle creates a hosted conda channel and a virtual over
# it, GETs the virtual's noarch/repodata.json (that runs a merge through the
# gate), then reads the line from `docker logs`.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL) both images: the virtual's repodata.json answers 200 and the
#             gate init line is in the log.
#   (FIX)     budget_bytes is 34359738368 and max_merges is greater than 3
#             (28 at the default member ceilings). RED on 1.11.4:
#             budget_bytes=4294967295, max_merges=3.
PROFILES="storage.filesystem limits.proxy-metadata-budget"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
export AK_PROXY_METADATA_BUDGET_BYTES="34359738368"
